{"ts": 1789715234.2863412, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted feature 35 on its own, long, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.2 (standard deviation 0.35). Its in-sample Sharpe of 0.625 did not clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.022, and no combination I tried did either. The best was 0.786.\n\n- **Looks like noise overall.** I evaluated all 40 features individually over the 5,000-day sample, which I took to be about 20 years of daily data. Their Sharpes spread with a standard deviation of about 0.195. Pure sampling noise alone would give about 0.224, so as a group the features show no evidence of signal.\n- **Feature 35 is the only outlier.** Its 0.625 is about 2.8 standard errors from zero, which is only modest evidence once you account for it being the best of 40.\n- **Combinations add nothing real.** I tried eight two- and three-feature combinations, all built around feature 35. The best, long features 35 and 30 and short feature 39, is what you get from averaging three independent noisy features. The gain over feature 35 alone is therefore more likely selection than signal.\n- **Why feature 35 alone.** If any of that 0.625 is real, adding features that are probably noise would dilute it. If none of it is real, the choice makes no difference.\n- **How I set the prediction.** I shrank the in-sample figure heavily toward zero to get the 0.2 mean. The 0.35 standard deviation covers both my uncertainty about the true Sharpe and sampling noise in the unseen data.\n\nI stopped searching after those combinations, since further search in a set that looks like noise would mostly raise the in-sample figure without improving the out-of-sample one."}
